# App Flow Document for Next.js MVP Starter Template

## Onboarding and Sign-In/Sign-Up

When a brand-new user arrives at the application, they are greeted by a clean landing page that briefly introduces the capabilities of the system and invites them to either log in or sign up. To create an account, the user clicks the Sign Up button, which takes them to a registration form powered by Better Auth. They enter their email address, choose a secure password, and submit the form. The system processes the information, creates a new user record in the PostgreSQL database using Drizzle ORM, and initiates a session. Once registration succeeds, the user is automatically logged in and redirected to the main dashboard. Existing users access the Sign In page by clicking the Log In button. They supply their email and password, and the template’s authentication API route verifies their credentials against the database. If the credentials are valid, the user is granted an active session token and taken to the home page. In case a user forgets their password, they click the “Forgot Password” link on the login page, which displays a form to request a reset. Although the template does not include email delivery by default, it provides a clear placeholder route where an email service can be integrated. After requesting a reset, the user receives an email with a link that leads them to a password reset form. Upon entering a new password and confirming it, the system updates their record, and the user can log in with the new credentials. When users wish to sign out, they click the Log Out button in the navigation bar. This action clears the session on both client and server sides, and the user is returned to the landing page.

## Main Dashboard or Home Page

After signing in, the user lands on the default dashboard view. At the top, a header displays the application name, a theme toggle button for light or dark mode, and the user’s avatar with a dropdown menu for profile and logout actions. On the left, a collapsible sidebar offers navigation links to key sections such as Dashboard Home, Settings, and any additional feature pages created by extending the template’s folder structure under `/app/dashboard`. The central area of the screen shows a welcome message, summary widgets for recent activity, and quick links to create new items or view existing data. Each widget and link is implemented as a reusable UI component from the shadcn/ui library, styled with Tailwind CSS. From this home page, clicking on any sidebar link or dashboard widget seamlessly transitions the user to the corresponding section via Next.js App Router client-side navigation.

## Detailed Feature Flows and Page Transitions

The core feature of the template is user authentication, but the same pattern applies to any future modules. To view or manage profile details, the user navigates to the Profile page by selecting “Profile” from the avatar dropdown. This page fetches user data on the server, renders a form prefilled with name and email fields, and allows the user to update their information. Saving changes triggers an API route that updates the Drizzle ORM schema in the database and then refreshes the page with a success message. For creating a new feature section, developers replicate the `/app/dashboard` directory structure. For example, adding a Products section involves creating `/app/dashboard/products/layout.tsx` and `/app/dashboard/products/page.tsx`. The user clicks “Products” in the sidebar, which loads the new layout and page component. On that page, list and create actions follow the same data-fetching and API-route pattern: server component retrieves existing items, client component renders a form, submission calls an API route to mutate the database, and the page refreshes. The theme toggle lives in the header. When toggled, it updates a client-side cookie that persists theme preference. The next server render reads this cookie to apply the correct Tailwind CSS class. Developers can also create admin-only sections by wrapping feature pages in an authorization check component that reads session details from `authClient`. Premium or role-based paths can branch by inspecting the user’s role in the session token before rendering a page.

## Settings and Account Management

Users manage personal information in the Settings area, accessible from the sidebar. The Settings page renders sections for updating email, changing passwords, and configuring notification preferences. Each section is a self-contained form that calls its own API route on submit. For billing or subscription flows, the Settings page provides placeholder routes where a payment integration (such as Stripe) can be plugged in. When users finish adjusting settings, they simply click the “Back to Dashboard” button in the page header, which uses Next.js navigation to return them to the home view without losing state. Throughout these settings pages, the consistent layout and UI library ensure a familiar experience that matches the rest of the app.

## Error States and Alternate Paths

If a user enters invalid credentials during login or registration, the form displays an inline error message below the input fields, explaining the problem in plain language. Client-side validation using HTML attributes prevents empty submissions, while server-side checks catch issues like duplicate email or weak passwords. During form submissions, if the network connection is lost or the server is unreachable, a toast notification alerts the user to check their connection and retry. API routes include try/catch blocks that return standardized error responses. The global error boundary defined in `/app/error.tsx` catches unexpected exceptions in React component trees and shows a user-friendly fallback page with a retry button. If a user tries to access a restricted page without proper authorization, they are redirected to a custom 403 page that explains they lack permission and provides a link back to the dashboard or login screen. When session tokens expire, the user is automatically redirected to the Sign In page with a message indicating their session has ended.

## Conclusion and Overall App Journey

From the first visit to everyday usage, the Next.js MVP Starter Template guides users through a smooth, intuitive process. New users sign up or log in, then arrive at a well-organized dashboard where they can immediately view critical information and navigate to different modules. Profile and settings pages let users manage personal details and preferences without leaving the main flow. Developers extend the application by creating new route directories under `/app/dashboard`, following the established pattern for server-rendered pages and client-side forms that call API routes. Throughout the experience, error handling and theme support ensure the app feels robust and polished. By following this flow document, anyone building on this template can understand exactly how each piece connects, from authentication to feature extension, providing a clear path from sign-up to a fully functional MVP.
